OBSERVATIONS (non-blocking; none affect the VERDICT above)

  1. PRE-EXISTING, real, dark-theme-only contrast bug (found by this
     sweep, not this run): whatif page, "Adjust spending rules manually"
     <summary> (web/templates/components/whatif/guardrails.html, 0-diff vs
     master). It carries no text-color class at all
     (`class="cursor-pointer rounded font-medium focus:outline
     focus:outline-2 focus:outline-accent"`), so it inherits the browser
     default black text. Its ancestor card is `bg-white dark:bg-gray-800`.
     In light theme that's black-on-white — fine. In dark theme axe
     measured fgColor #000000 on bgColor #292524 = 1.38:1 (need 4.5:1) —
     axe rule color-contrast, impact "serious". This is exactly the
     ACCESSIBILITY.md A-2 failure mode in its own words ("a control
     readable in one theme and invisible in the other is a FAIL, not a
     nitpick") — genuinely fails A-2/A-3, but predates this run entirely
     (guardrails.html and whatif.html are both byte-identical to master
     86d3a7c; no IM/IM1/IM1T/IM2 commit touches whatif.html,
     components/whatif/, or any CSS). Recommend a backlog task: add e.g.
     `text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100` (or the sitewide heading-color
     token) to that <summary>'s class list. Confirmed not a JS-driven
     state — no script in web/static/js/ references this element's class
     or color; the composed page's forced class="dark" reproduces exactly
     what the real boot script would leave on <html> in a real browser.

  2. Same pre-existing axe "incomplete" nav-logo item IM2's checker already
